    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 8, 8]  ->  2\nnums = [3, 3, 7, 7, 10, 11, 11]    ->  10\nnums = [5]                         ->  5"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: the pairs shift at the single value"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The array has an odd length, and pairs fill it two by two. Until the single value appears, every pair starts at an **even** index: `nums[0] == nums[1]`, `nums[2] == nums[3]`, …. The single value pushes everything after it one place right, so from there on pairs start at **odd** indexes. Take an **even** `mid`: if `nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]`, the pairs are still in step, so the single value is beyond this pair — `low = mid + 2`. If not, the shift already happened: the single value is at `mid` or before — `high = mid`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. XOR everything"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "`a ^ a = 0` and `0 ^ b = b`, so XOR-ing every value cancels the pairs and leaves the single one. It works on unsorted input too."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function singleNonDuplicate(nums) {\n  let x = 0;\n  for (const v of nums) x ^= v;   // pairs cancel out\n  return x;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def single_non_duplicate(nums):\n    x = 0\n    for v in nums:\n        x ^= v                            # pairs cancel out\n    return x"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    int x = 0;\n    for (int v : nums) x ^= v;            // pairs cancel out\n    return x;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int singleNonDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int x = 0;\n    for (int v : nums) x ^= v;            // pairs cancel out\n    return x;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).** Neat, but it ignores the sorting and misses the O(log n) requirement."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Walk the pairs"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function singleNonDuplicate(nums) {\n  for (let i = 0; i + 1 < nums.length; i += 2) {\n    if (nums[i] !== nums[i + 1]) return nums[i];   // the first broken pair starts with the single one\n  }\n  return nums[nums.length - 1];                    // no broken pair: it's the last value\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def single_non_duplicate(nums):\n    for i in range(0, len(nums) - 1, 2):\n        if nums[i] != nums[i + 1]:\n            return nums[i]                # the first broken pair starts with the single one\n    return nums[-1]                       # no broken pair: it's the last value"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < nums.length; i += 2) {\n        if (nums[i] != nums[i + 1]) return nums[i];   // the first broken pair starts with the single one\n    }\n    return nums[nums.length - 1];                     // no broken pair: it's the last value\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int singleNonDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < (int)nums.size(); i += 2) {\n        if (nums[i] != nums[i + 1]) return nums[i];   // the first broken pair starts with the single one\n    }\n    return nums.back();                               // no broken pair: it's the last value\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).** This is the idea binary search speeds up: find the **first broken pair**."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Binary search on even indexes"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "single-element",
      "caption": "Look only at even indexes: an intact pair there means the single value is further right."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function singleNonDuplicate(nums) {\n  let low = 0;\n  let high = nums.length - 1;\n  while (low < high) {\n    let mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);\n    if (mid % 2 === 1) mid--;                        // look at the start of a pair: an even index\n    if (nums[mid] === nums[mid + 1]) low = mid + 2;  // pair intact: the single one is further right\n    else high = mid;                                 // pair broken: the single one is at mid or before\n  }\n  return nums[low];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def single_non_duplicate(nums):\n    low, high = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if mid % 2 == 1:\n            mid -= 1                      # look at the start of a pair: an even index\n        if nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]:\n            low = mid + 2                 # pair intact: the single one is further right\n        else:\n            high = mid                    # pair broken: the single one is at mid or before\n    return nums[low]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {\n    int low = 0, high = nums.length - 1;\n    while (low < high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (mid % 2 == 1) mid--;                         // look at the start of a pair: an even index\n        if (nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) low = mid + 2;   // pair intact: the single one is further right\n        else high = mid;                                 // pair broken: the single one is at mid or before\n    }\n    return nums[low];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int singleNonDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int low = 0, high = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (low < high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (mid % 2 == 1) mid--;                         // look at the start of a pair: an even index\n        if (nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) low = mid + 2;   // pair intact: the single one is further right\n        else high = mid;                                 // pair broken: the single one is at mid or before\n    }\n    return nums[low];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(log n), space O(1).** `low` and `high` stay even (both start even and move by even amounts), and the answer is always at an even index, so the range always holds it. `mid + 1` is always a valid index because `mid < high`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "XOR everything",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "Every value is **XORed once**; pairs cancel out.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "One running XOR."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Walk the pairs",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "It checks pairs until the **first broken pair**.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Just an index."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Binary search on even indexes",
          "time": "O(log n)",
          "timeWhy": "Whether the pair at `mid` is intact tells which **half** holds the single value.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Only `low` and `high`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**One value** — `[5]`.",
        "**Single value first** — `[1, 2, 2]`.",
        "**Single value last** — `[1, 1, 2]`.",
        "**Negative numbers and zero** — XOR and comparisons both still work."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Single Number** — unsorted, every other value twice: XOR in O(n).",
        "**Single Number II** — every other value three times: count each bit modulo 3.",
        "**Find the Duplicate Number** — binary search on values 1..n, counting how many are at most mid."
      ]
    }
  ],
